WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.910
Okay, so we are getting winked and.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:13.550
We play E4, and of course, we play our

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.030
favorite Aleppan Sicilian.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.210
Let's see how the Aleppan holds up at a

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:19.790
very high level, at a 2 ,000 plus level,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:20.470
Knight F6.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.850
As I said, at this level, we're

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.550
essentially exclusively facing the two

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.110
main responses to the Aleppine.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.870
The first of which is D5.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.230
We face that move a lot.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.270
We haven't faced Knight F6 as much.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:33.890
So this is a great opportunity for us

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.670
to review some of the theory of this

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.930
line and for me to show some new,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.790
exciting analysis.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.910
So after Knight D5, we already kind of

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.550
need to get into the weeds because

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.390
there are essentially two.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.280
fundamentally different ways of playing

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:47.920
the Aleppan.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.600
The first is the more traditional

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.040
approach, which is to play D4.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.200
this position.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.520
This is what you might have seen.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.840
Then Black plays pawn takes d4.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.160
And notice that in that resulting

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.600
position, White doesn't have to

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.100
recapture on d4 because d takes c3 is

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:04.880
not a threat.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.560
The queen x -rays the knight.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.980
So the main line is d4, pawn takes d4,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.740
and then actually knight f3 is a very

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:12.540
important inclusion.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.220
But recently, Aleppine experts have

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.420
embarked on a fundamentally different

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.480
approach, a more exciting approach that

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.140
leads to far more tactical positions.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.660
Rather than playing d4, It is worth

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.280
starting with the move Knight F3.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.740
In many cases, this transposes to the

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.400
main line, generally Black plays Knight c6.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.970
then you might think that white has to

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.510
play d4 black plays pawn takes pawn And

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.490
we have ourselves the main line of the

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.510
Allopan, but...

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.190
After Knight c6, there is a second

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.230
approach, and this is the approach I'd

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.730
like to introduce you to in this game,

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.730
assuming that our opponent plays Knight

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:48.950
c6, which is not a given.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.070
Black can also play d6.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.090
Black can also play e6.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.960
And those are the three main moves.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.120
Knight c6 is on the board.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.380
Does anybody know what move I'm implying?

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:00.280
This may seem.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.330
Okay, depending on how you look at this

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.510
move, it may seem very natural, but

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.530
upon closer examination, this move may seem.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.840
to be a serious positional mistake.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.320
I'm talking, of course, about Bishop to C4.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.080
We bring our Bishop out straight away.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.700
We hit the Knight on D5.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.300
and we're essentially forcing the

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.820
knight to move back to b6 or black can

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.070
also support the knight with e6 which

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.230
is a very popular move.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.050
I don't think it's a very good line for black.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.870
I am really hoping to see e6.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.470
I think those positions are really,

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:32.870
really fun to play for white.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.650
But so are the main lines after an ITP6.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.390
In which case, we drop our bishop back

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:39.850
to b3.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.590
Why did I say that this may seem like a serious?

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.020
positional mistake.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.840
Well, we're actually encouraging black

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.540
to play a very scary looking move here.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.870
Our opponent didn't play it, and that's

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:51.790
the move c4.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:55.550
And C4 is scary because if we then play

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.250
D4 at any later point, Black can en

00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.890
passant and break up our pawn chain.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.830
So it may seem like we're going to end

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.430
up losing our E5 pawn.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:05.370
And I'll show you some of the theory

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.490
after C4 based on the new.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.920
Svidler Chasuble course, as well as the

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:11.820
Kalofman Bible.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.720
And those lines are incredibly fascinating.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.560
What you will see.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.460
is that we often end up sacrificing the

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:19.180
e5 pawn.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:22.290
And we go for rapid fire play on the

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:23.450
queen side and in the center.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.030
And I think this line has breathed a

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.530
lot of new life into the Allopan.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:28.580
So...

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.510
Some exciting stuff coming up after

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.630
this game, but D5 is the traditional

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:33.230
main line.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.790
This is how people used to play this exclusively.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:36.940
Uh, you know.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.540
15, 20 years ago.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.140
And here we need to not miss the

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.260
opportunity to take on Passant.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.320
If we don't take on Passant, Black gets

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.600
like an ideal setup.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.170
by bringing their bishop out to G4.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.470
So without question, we need to take

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.310
Ampersand and keep the diagonal for

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.190
this bishop open.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.130
And now, we need to quickly develop our pieces.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:57.230
You may.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.140
be attracted to the possibility of the

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.040
immediate D4.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.340
but we're not really aiming to play a

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:04.860
quick D4.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.120
That's not the point of this particular line.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.360
The point of this line is to quickly

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.180
develop your pieces and actually go

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:12.740
after the B6 knight.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.910
And you might say, oh, after the b6

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.390
knight, how on earth is that possible?

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.890
Well, there is a very important idea

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.170
and by carrying it out, you're lighting

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.510
the fire on the queen side.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.790
But first, first things first, we need

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.070
to get our king out of the center.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.890
And you need to realize that Bishop g4

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.230
is an incredibly common blunder in this position.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.350
It walks right into the common tactic.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:35.850
Bishop takes f7 check.

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:36.370
I've won.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.770
countless games like that, even against

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:39.610
other GMs.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.840
And after Kxf7, it's the classic Ng5

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.840
check, we pick up the bishop with interest.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.460
Bb6 is the main line.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.750
And this is where things get really interesting.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.090
Traditionally, the main line has always

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:53.310
been Night A3.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:54.870
And if you've watched the Aleppine

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.990
speedrun videos to date, you will know

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.810
that Night A3 is a very.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.620
Classic developing move because the

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.160
Knight aims directly for B5.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.560
So that's what people used to play all

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:04.880
the time.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.060
And then recently, a new idea has taken

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.320
the Aleppine by storm.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.300
Here's what it involves.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:12.940
First of all, we trade bishops on e6,

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.040
and we do that in order to buy

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.360
ourselves a couple of tempi.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:17.580
What does that mean?

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:18.940
Well, we're making it difficult for

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.500
Black to develop his king side, right?

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.060
Black would love to play E6 on the next

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:23.540
move, but.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.550
He can, he's got to move the queen

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.570
away, and that costs black a temp, or

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.470
black has to fianchetto the bishop, but

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:30.490
that's two more tempi.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:32.930
And we use these tempi to develop...

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.500
Rapid play on the queen side who can

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.200
tell me what idea I've been implying

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.650
for a while now It's not D4, D4 is

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:43.670
again, the traditional way to play.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.590
We go after Black's knight on b6 with

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.690
the move a4.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:50.830
An idea that you should be familiar with.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:53.440
because it's universally applied in

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.900
many different types of positions.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:58.030
We're shoving this pawn down to A5.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:05:59.900
You might look at this and say, well, I

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.520
don't get how this is dangerous, right?

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.740
The move a5 is just a one move thread

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:05.840
and the knight has plenty of retreating squares.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:07.000
Hopefully.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.160
The course of the game will reveal why

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.400
A5 is actually a dangerous idea, and

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.180
it's not because the knight on B6 is

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.180
somehow in trouble.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.220
It's because we're trying to push this

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.080
pawn all the way up to a6 and create

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.180
long -term weaknesses.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.410
for black on the queen side.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:25.630
In particular, some of the light

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.310
squares are gonna become weak.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.090
And believe it or not, the pawn on a seven.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.140
is going to become a long -term weakness

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:34.980
once it is fixed in place.

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.950
by our pawn on a6 of course if black

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.210
plays a5 here which is a legitimate

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.340
move then the knight on b6 loses its defender.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.930
Black creates a gaping weakness on B5,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.250
which we can immediately occupy with

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:49.630
our knight.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.870
And at the right moment, we will play

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.850
d4 and blast open the center, and there

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.750
might be hell to pay for Black if he

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.410
makes too many weaknesses on the queen side.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:00.790
Yeah, of course, Rookie 1 is a move that...

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.010
Most of you are gonna be tempted to

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.450
play, but remember the sort of

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:05.710
philosophy behind this line.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.910
It's actually a good thing that the

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:08.670
queen is on e6.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.670
It's making it difficult for black to

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.310
get the king side pieces out.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.470
So, Rook e1 would actually help Black

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.990
by forcing the Queen back to d7.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.630
We don't want to play Rook e1.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.120
unless there's a compelling reason to

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:22.520
do so.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:25.620
Very instructive point, by the way, right?

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:27.680
You can see how, at this level, these

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.080
tempting -looking moves are actually

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:30.840
often bad.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.220
Queen d7, so our opponent is abiding by

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:37.020
the sort of clear strategy of kingside development.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.460
That means we have no time to waste.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.320
We need to continue our queenside

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.900
campaign with a5.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:43.310
And this is still theory.

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.130
Queen d7 is, I believe, the main line.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.250
And don't worry, we are going to play d4.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.150
We're just waiting for the perfect

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.210
opportunity to do that.

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:52.930
Nighty -five.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.880
So we need to complete the picture on

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.280
the queen side, what we do not want to allow.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.340
is we don't want to allow black to play

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:01.560
a6 themselves.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.950
because then our pawn on a5 becomes a

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.210
very serious liability.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.130
It's a liability because, well, because

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.050
the knight on c6 aims at the pawn and

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:11.950
we don't want black to freeze the queen

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.690
side before we've done our job.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.070
We want to freeze the queen side on our terms.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.270
on our terms, which means we want to

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:20.170
throw in the move A6.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.940
And as I've explained, we're trying to

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:24.240
induce the move B6.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.880
And it may not seem like the weak light

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.160
squares on the queen side are

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.900
accessible because we don't have a

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:30.960
light squared bishop anymore.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.480
That bishop got traded for its

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.300
counterpart on E6, but there's a piece

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.160
called a queen and queens also move diagonally.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.000
So the queen is going to be ultimately

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.540
the vehicle that we're going to use to

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.200
try to exploit the queen side light

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:45.860
square weaknesses.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:48.790
I'm anticipating the move B6.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.850
If Black doesn't play B6, then we will

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.050
take on B7 and shatter Black's entire queenside.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.870
If Black plays Bxa6, the same thing

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:57.590
applies, and...

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.920
Black merely invites our Rook onto a

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:00.960
super active square.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:02.240
B6 is played.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.070
now again we need to be very accurate

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:07.770
because if we fall asleep then black is

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.570
going to play e6 and bishop e7 and

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:12.910
these squares look very nice but we we

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.250
actually need to develop the machinery

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.050
in order to exploit them.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:19.150
In particular, we need to combine

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:21.950
positional play on the queen side with

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:23.250
a general approach.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:26.290
that involves making it as difficult as

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:28.430
possible for Black to develop his bishop.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:30.130
Right, we want.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.300
black to remain frozen in place on the

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.920
king side because it's going to make it

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.920
difficult for black to protect some of

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.920
these weak squares and the weak a7 pawn

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.460
on the queen side.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.440
This means that it's the perfect

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:44.200
opportunity for us to strike in the

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.680
center with the move d4.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.090
And this might be the last opportunity

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:48.910
that we're going to get.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.030
So we need to jump on the opportunity train.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.390
before it's left the station, D4.

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.020
Opening up the center, and not forcing

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.140
Black to take on d4, Black I guess can

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:00.760
also play the move e6 here.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:02.950
We'll cross that bridge if we get there.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:05.980
But the point, and hopefully you're.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.800
starting to form a clearer picture of

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.380
what it is we're trying to accomplish here.

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:12.790
If black takes on d4, which is what

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.070
most people will play, then we take

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:15.870
back with the knight.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.120
And if black takes, we take back with

